#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# permute

#% text
# `permute(xs, p)` gives the list xs with its element i moved to position p[i], for a permutation list p or a permutation in cycles form.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A permutation list shorter than xs leaves the remaining positions in place. `cycles([[1, 3, 2]])` moves element 1 to position 3, 3 to 2 and 2 to 1.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
permute([a, b, c, d], [1, 4, 3, 2])
#% output
#= [a, d, c, b]

#% cell
fold(permute, [a, b, c, d], [[1, 4, 3, 2], [3, 2, 4, 1]])
#% output
#= [b, d, a, c]

#% cell
permute([a, b, c, d, e], cycles([[1, 3, 2]]))
#% output
#= [b, c, a, d, e]

#% text
# Moving each element to its position in the ordering sorts the list:

#% cell
{ let xs = [5, 3, 9, 1]; permute(xs, ordering(ordering(xs))) }
#% output
#= [1, 3, 5, 9]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `permutation_list`, `cycles`, `ordering`
